module

public import Sheaf.RingTheory.CompleteIntersectionOverField
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.TensorProduct.Basic

/-!
# Local complete intersections and extension of the base field

Source: Stacks Project, Algebra, Lemma 10.135.11 (Tag 00SJ)
(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-lci-field-change).
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u v w

open scoped TensorProduct

namespace Algebra

/-- (blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-lci-field-change) Stacks Project, Algebra, Lemma 10.135.11
(Tag 00SJ). The right-factor algebra structure makes `S ⊗[k] K` a `K`-algebra by
`λ ↦ 1 ⊗ₜ λ`. It is of finite type, and being a local complete intersection is invariant
under this extension of the base field.

Proof (the five steps of the DAG node):
1. Choose a finite polynomial presentation `P → S` with kernel `I`. The polynomial ring
   is Noetherian, so `I` is finitely generated. Presentation base change identifies the
   tensor product with `K[x₁, ..., xₙ]/I'`, where `I'` is generated by the images of `I`.
   This proves finite type over `K` (lem-weil-pbc-presentation-base-change and
   lem-weil-pbc-polynomial-ring-field-regular-ring).
2. Exactness of tensoring with the field `K` identifies `I'/I'²` with
   `(I/I²) ⊗[S] (S ⊗[k] K)`. Apply exactness to `I² ⊆ I ⊆ P`, identify the base-changed
   polynomial ring and its ideals, and use tensor associativity to obtain the stated
   conormal identification (lem-weil-field-scalar-extension-exact).
3. For a prime `q` of the tensor product and its contraction `p`, dimension at the point
   is unchanged (lem-weil-pbc-dimension-at-point-field-extension). Localizing the
   conormal identification gives base change from `S_p` to `(S ⊗[k] K)_q`. Passing to
   residue fields preserves the dimension of the conormal fibre.
4. If `S` is locally a complete intersection, its localized conormal module at `p` is
   free of rank `n - dim_p Spec S` (lem-weil-pbc-lci-conormal-free-rank-codimension).
   Its base change at `q` has that many generators. The unchanged dimension and
   lem-weil-pbc-lci-conormal-generators-global-complete-intersection give a global
   complete intersection on a principal neighbourhood of `q`. These opens cover.
5. Conversely, choose `q` over each `p`, using surjectivity of spectra under base change
   (lem-weil-pbc-spec-surjective-base-change). The conormal fibre at `q` has dimension
   `n - dim_p Spec S`. Step 3 gives the same dimension at `p`, and Nakayama lifts a basis
   to generators (lem-weil-pbc-nakayama-local-generators). The conormal generator
   criterion gives a global complete intersection on a principal neighbourhood of
   `p`; these neighbourhoods cover `Spec S`. -/
theorem isLocalCompleteIntersection_fieldChange
    (k : Type u) (K : Type v) (S : Type w) [Field k] [Field K] [CommRing S]
    [Algebra k K] [Algebra k S] [Algebra.FiniteType k S] :
    letI := Algebra.TensorProduct.rightAlgebra (R := k) (A := S) (B := K)
    ∃ h : Algebra.FiniteType K (S ⊗[k] K),
      letI := h
      IsLocalCompleteIntersection k S ↔ IsLocalCompleteIntersection K (S ⊗[k] K) := by
  sorry

end Algebra
